-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.x -> 5.x (PHP 8.3 modernization)

1. Backward Incompatible Changes
2. New Features

========================================
1. Backward Incompatible Changes
========================================

- PHP: minimum supported version is now `>= 8.3` (was `>= 8.1`).
- `BackQ\Adapter\Nsq` is **removed**, and with it the in-repo NSQ TCP protocol
  implementation. The adapter was deprecated back in 2.0.6 and has shipped as
  beta without a maintainer since; 5.x drops it rather than carry it untested.
  Gone from the release: the `BackQ\Adapter\Nsq` class, `example/adapter/nsq/`
  and every `example/**/nsq.php` pair, the `nsq` service in
  `build/docker-compose.yaml`, the `BACKQ_NSQD_HOST` / `BACKQ_NSQD_PORT`
  environment variables, and the NSQ test suite. Workers, publishers and
  messages written against NSQ need no rewrite beyond the adapter they are
  handed: `AbstractAdapter` is the contract they program to, so passing a
  `Beanstalk` or `Redis` instance is the whole migration. To keep using NSQ,
  stay on the 4.x line.
- **The whole AWS surface is removed**: the `DynamoSQS` adapter, the
  `Amazon\SNS` push-notification feature, and the `aws/aws-sdk-php`
  dependency. Every AWS class is gone —

  | Removed | Was |
  |---|---|
  | `BackQ\Adapter\DynamoSQS` | the DynamoDB + SQS queue adapter |
  | `BackQ\Adapter\Amazon\DynamoDb\QueueTableRow` | its DynamoDB row helper |
  | `BackQ\Message\Amazon\SNS\Application\PlatformEndpoint\*` | the `Publish` / `Register` / `Remove` messages and their interfaces |
  | `BackQ\Publisher\Amazon\SNS\Application\PlatformEndpoint\*` | the matching publishers |
  | `BackQ\Worker\Amazon\SNS\*` | the `SnsClient`, its exceptions, and the `Application` / `PlatformEndpoint` workers |

  Also gone: `example/adapter/dynamosqs/` and `example/workers/amazon/`, their
  test suites, and the `aws`, `dynamodb`, `sqs`, `apns`, `fcm` and `apple`
  Packagist keywords. Nothing in the library needs AWS any more, so
  `aws/aws-sdk-php` is no longer installed for you — a meaningful dependency
  reduction.

  The SNS workers were **push** workers, not queue consumers: they published to
  an SNS endpoint ARN and never touched DynamoDB, so they were independent of
  `DynamoSQS` and were dropped as a separate feature. Replace them with the
  provider APIs directly (FCM HTTP v1, APNs HTTP/2). To keep any of this, stay
  on the 4.x line.

  Migration for the `DynamoSQS` adapter: it was the only backend providing
  reliable long-delay scheduling (jobs waited as DynamoDB items driven by TTL
  plus a Lambda stream trigger). The [`Serialized`
  worker](README.md#the-serialized-worker-a-proxy-worker) covers the same need
  on any surviving adapter.
- `guzzlehttp/guzzle` is now a **direct** dependency (`^7`), where it was
  previously pulled in only as a transitive dependency of `aws/aws-sdk-php`.
  `BackQ\Worker\Guzzle` and `BackQ\Worker\GuzzleForwarder` use
  `GuzzleHttp\Client`, so dropping the AWS SDK would otherwise have removed a
  package the library imports directly. No action needed if you already relied
  on the transitive copy.
- `BackQ\Adapter\AbstractAdapter::hasWorkers($queue)` is narrowed to `bool`
  (was `bool|int|null`). Concrete adapters return real values where the backend
  supports the check and `false` elsewhere: `Redis` answers from a per-queue
  lease registry the workers write (see below), `Beanstalk` implements the check
  against real tube stats, and `MySql` answers from a companion worker table (also
  see below). Subclasses overriding `hasWorkers()` must declare a `bool` return
  type.

  **`Redis::hasWorkers()` is no longer the stub it was in 4.x.** It performs Redis
  I/O, so it can now log an `error` where it was previously silent, and it answers
  "was a worker seen on this queue within `workerTtl` seconds" rather than a
  constant `false`. Code that branched on the constant still gets a `false` it can
  publish on, and no signature changed: the answer moves from always-false to a
  real answer, not from one answer to another. `RedisConfig` gained an eleventh
  field, `workerTtl` (default `300`, minimum `5`), appended last, so every existing
  construction — positional or named — keeps working.

  **`MySql::hasWorkers()` is no longer the stub it was in 4.x either.** It counts
  the rows of a companion `backq_workers` table, one per bound worker, answering
  the same sentence Redis does. MySQL shares one job table between every queue, so
  the registry carries a `queue` column of its own; that is the only way "is a
  worker on *this* queue" can be asked at all.

  ```sql
  CREATE TABLE `backq_workers` (
    `id` int unsigned NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
    `queue` varchar(64) NOT NULL,
    `token` varbinary(16) NOT NULL,
    `seen` datetime NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (`id`),
    UNIQUE KEY `backq_workers_token` (`token`),
    KEY `backq_workers_queue_seen` (`queue`, `seen`),
    KEY `backq_workers_seen` (`seen`)
  ) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4;
  ```

  Both secondary indexes are load-bearing and neither duplicates the other. The
  count filters on a queue *and* a time, so it needs `(queue, seen)`. The reap
  filters on the time alone — it is the one statement in the feature that is not
  scoped to a queue — so it needs `(seen)`; drop that index and the same `DELETE`
  becomes a full scan *and* a `Using filesort` on every renew interval, measured
  on 8.0.46. If you are creating the table from an older copy of this file, add
  the second key.

  **`token` is `varbinary(16)`, and that is not only a size.** It holds a version
  7 UUID, the adapter sends it as `UNHEX()` of the 32 hex characters, and a
  `utf8mb4` `varchar` rejects 16 raw bytes with error 1366 — measured — so a
  table created from an older copy of this DDL does not merely carry a wider
  index, it rejects the statement. `ALTER TABLE … MODIFY token varbinary(16)`
  does not convert the old rows, so a table that is already in use has to be
  emptied and re-leased; the leases are disposable, they expire on their own. The
  id is time-based, so `SELECT HEX(token)` says when a lease was minted, and it is
  not monotonic: within one millisecond the order is decided by the random bits.

  **This is the one feature in the library that needs DDL.** The adapter never
  runs it: a deployment that has not created the table answers "no workers" and
  logs one `debug` line and never raises — once per adapter, so once for the life of a
  long-running worker, and once per request under PHP-FPM, where keeping `debug` out of
  production is what bounds the noise. `JobConfig` gained two
  optional trailing arguments, `string $workerTable` and `int $workerTtl`
  (default `300`, minimum `JobConfig::WORKER_TTL_MIN`, 5, below which the
  constructor now throws an `InvalidArgumentException` as `RedisConfig` already
  did), so `workerTtl` means one thing across the library. They are appended last
  because `JobConfig` is `final readonly` and existing code constructs it
  positionally.

  Set `workerTtl` above your longest job, for the same reason as on Redis and with
  the same consequence: a worker mid-job is not renewing, so the answer is `false`
  — publish anyway — rather than a wrong `true`. A worker that was `kill -9`'d
  stops being counted when its lease expires; its row stays until a worker reaps
  it, which is the operational difference from the Redis registry. The reap runs
  on the renew's `workerTtl / 3` schedule and not only when a worker starts: a
  worker that binds once and then runs for months never starts again, so a reap
  that lived only in `bindRead()` would leave one row per crash behind forever.
  It deletes what is three lease lengths past expiry, oldest first, 1000 rows at a
  time, and a lease that cannot be written does not stop it running. A publisher
  only reads, and a publisher never appears in the count.
- **The process worker no longer writes to the PHP error log.** `BackQ\Worker\AProcess`
  reports unsupported payloads, child-launch failures, fork-cleanup failures,
  timeouts, signals and outer-loop failures through the PSR-3 logger set with
  `setLogger()`, at level `error`, instead of `@error_log()`. Code that watched
  `php.ini`'s `error_log` destination (journald, a web-server error log, `php-fpm`)
  for these messages must switch to a PSR-3 handler, or pass a file-writing
  logger to `setLogger()`.

  `logError()` is a no-op when no logger is set, so `setLogger(null)` now
  silences these messages completely — where `error_log()` used to keep
  reporting them. By default they land in the `ConsoleLogger` that
  `AbstractWorker::__construct()` installs, so under supervisor or
  `docker logs` they are still visible.

  `AProcess` was the only remaining `error_log()` caller in 5.x: the NSQ and
  `Amazon\SNS` workers that shared the pattern are removed, and
  `src/Worker/Guzzle.php` never had one. `build/phpcs-ruleset.xml` now forbids
  the `error_log` function outright, so it cannot come back.
- `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::logError()` gained an optional
  `array $context = []` second parameter, forwarded to
  `Psr\Log\LoggerInterface::error()`. The former `@error_log()` call sites now
  pass the caught exception as `['exception' => $e]`, so a PSR-3 handler gets the
  type, the code and the `previous` chain instead of a concatenated message
  string. Subclasses overriding `logError(string $message)` are unaffected (the
  parameter is optional and nothing in the library overrides the method), but a
  subclass that declared a *narrower* signature must match. `logInfo()` and
  `logDebug()` are unchanged.
- **The adapter logger is mandatory and the adapter no longer wraps it.**
  `BackQ\Adapter\AbstractAdapter::__construct(?LoggerInterface $logger = null)`
  becomes `__construct(LoggerInterface $logger)`: an adapter cannot be built
  without a logger, and `setLogger()` is **removed** (it was `@deprecated` in the
  4.x → 5.x cycle below — pass the logger to the constructor instead). The
  `logInfo()`, `logDebug()` and `logError()` helpers are **removed** with it: an
  adapter that is handed a PSR-3 logger logs through it directly
  (`$this?->logger->error($message)`) instead of wrapping it in three
  pass-throughs, and the `isset()` guard each helper carried is gone with them.
  `build/phpcs-ruleset.xml` excludes
  `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.DisallowNullSafeObjectOperator` so
  that form is allowed in the adapters.

  The logger is the **first** constructor argument of every adapter, so a caller
  that never passed one has nothing to move:

  ```php
  new Beanstalk($logger);
  new MySqlAdapter($db, new JobConfig(), $logger);

  // was: new Redis($host, $port, logger: $logger)
  // now: new Redis($logger, new RedisConfig(host: $host, port: $port))
  new Redis($logger, new RedisConfig(host: $host, port: $port));
  ```

  The `Redis` constructor is the one signature that moves twice: its nine connection
  settings used to come first, with the logger appended as the tenth parameter, and
  a required parameter cannot follow optional ones — PHP 8 treats that as deprecated.
  So the logger moved to the front, and then the nine settings moved into
  `RedisConfig`, which keeps every name and default. Passing them as named arguments
  therefore still works, spelled as a `RedisConfig` argument — see the second
  `Redis::__construct()` entry further down for the full before/after table.

  Pass `new NullLogger()` (or any PSR-3 logger of your own) where the old
  `null` default meant "log nothing":

  ```php
  use Psr\Log\NullLogger;

  $adapter = new Beanstalk(new NullLogger());
  ```

  Migration:

  | Removed from `AbstractAdapter` | Use instead |
  |---|---|
  | `$adapter->logInfo($m)` | `$logger->info($m)` |
  | `$adapter->logDebug($m)` | `$logger->debug($m)` |
  | `$adapter->logError($m)` | `$logger->error($m)` |
  | `$adapter->setLogger($l)` | `new Beanstalk($l)` |

  A custom adapter that declared its own constructor must pass the logger on:
  `parent::__construct($logger)`. Code that subclassed `AbstractAdapter` and
  called the inherited `logError()` / `logDebug()` / `logInfo()` helpers must call
  the injected logger itself. The worker-side
  `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::setLogger()`, `logInfo()`, `logDebug()` and
  `logError()` are **not** affected: the worker's logger is still optional and
  still assigned through the setter.
- `BackQ\Adapter\AbstractAdapter` method parameters are now typed (were untyped):
  - `bindRead(string $queue)`, `bindWrite(string $queue)`,
    `hasWorkers(string $queue)`
  - `pickTask(?int $timeout = null)`
  - `putTask(string|Stringable $body)` — see the `putTask()` contract below
  - `afterWorkSuccess(?string $workId)`, `afterWorkFailed(?string $workId)` —
    typed `int|string|null` in the first pass and narrowed afterwards, see "The
    job id is acknowledged as a string" below
  - `ping(bool $reconnect = true)`
  Subclasses overriding these methods and callers must match the new argument
  types.
- `putTask()` takes one parameter, not an options array, and reports failure as a
  `Throwable` instead of `false`:

  ```php
  abstract public function putTask(string|Stringable $body): null|string|Throwable;
  ```

  Each adapter widens that signature by appending its own **optional** parameters.
  PHP allows this; it rejects a required extra parameter, a narrowed parameter
  type, and `false` added to the return union, all three as an incompatible
  declaration. Read the signature to learn what an adapter accepts.

  | Adapter | signature | result |
  |---|---|---|
  | `Beanstalk` | `putTask($body, int $readyWait = 0, ?int $jobTtr = null, ?int $priority = null)` | `string\|Throwable` |
  | `Redis` | `putTask($body, int $readyWait = 0)` | `string\|Throwable` |
  | `MySql` | `putTask($body, int\|string\|null $jobId = null, bool $putAsDone = false, bool $noSleep = false)` | `string\|Throwable` |

  Migration:

  ```php
  // was: $publisher->publish($message, [AbstractAdapter::PARAM_READYWAIT => 5]);
  $publisher->publish($message, readyWait: 5);
  $publisher->publish($message, ...$options);   // $options = ['readyWait' => 5]
  ```

  - **The `$params` array is gone, with no compatibility shim.** A wrong name is
    now reported by the engine as `Error: Unknown named parameter $jobttr`.
  - `AbstractPublisher::publish()` is variadic and forwards named arguments to
    `putTask()`. It throws `InvalidArgumentException` if an options array is
    passed, so the old form fails with a migration message instead of a
    confusing `TypeError` deep in the stack.
  - `publish()` before `start()` **returns** a `RuntimeException` (was
    `false`). It is a failure like any other, and `publish()` already has a
    channel for it. It does not throw, so a single `try` around the call covers
    every failure mode.
  - `false` is no longer a put result anywhere. A transport or storage failure
    comes back as the `Throwable`, so the type, message and previous chain
    survive. An **invalid argument still throws** (an out-of-range `jobTtr`, a
    `$readyWait` above `max_req_timeout`, a missing `MySql` job id), because
    that is a bug in the caller.
  - **A `Throwable` is truthy.** `if ($publisher->publish($m))` now reads a
    failure as success. Test `instanceof Throwable` instead:

    ```php
    $result = $publisher->publish($message);
    if ($result instanceof Throwable) {
        $logger->error('publish failed: ' . $result->getMessage(), ['exception' => $result]);
    }
    ```

  - `Message\Serialized::$publishOptions` is still an array (the payload is
    serialized into the queue) but is now a **named-argument map**, replayed
    with a splat: `new Serialized($message, $publisher, ['readyWait' => 5])`.
    **A delayed job already in a queue when you upgrade no longer replays**: its
    payload holds the retired `jobttr` / `readywait` keys and the worker logs
    `Error: Unknown named parameter`. Drain or purge the `serialized` queue
    across the upgrade.
  - `BackQ\Publisher\Serialized`'s default queue name is now `serialized` (was
    `mydynamodbtablenameandsqsqueuename`, a placeholder from when DynamoDB + SQS
    was the adapter it was written for). Anyone relying on the old default must
    pass the queue name explicitly.
  - `MySql::putTask()` `$jobId` is nullable only because PHP forbids appending a
    required parameter to an inherited method; a call without a usable id returns
    an `InvalidArgumentException`. Callers that always hold an id should call the
    new `MySql::putTaskTo(int|string $jobId, string|Stringable $body, bool $putAsDone = false, bool $noSleep = false)`.
  - Constants removed, all replaced by the parameter of the same meaning:
    `AbstractAdapter::PARAM_JOBTTR`, `AbstractAdapter::PARAM_READYWAIT`,
    `Beanstalk::PARAM_PRIORITY`. (`DynamoSQS::PARAM_MESSAGE_ID` went with the
    adapter.)
  - `AbstractAdapter::JOBTTR_DEFAULT` is removed. A TTR default is adapter
    configuration, not part of the base contract, so the adapter that has one
    declares it: `Beanstalk::JOBTTR_DEFAULT`, which seeds the `$jobTtr` default
    of `putTask()`. It is `60`, and code that read the constant off the base
    class, `AbstractAdapter::JOBTTR_DEFAULT`, now reads
    `Beanstalk::JOBTTR_DEFAULT`. `Beanstalk::PRIORITY_DEFAULT` stays and now
    seeds the `$priority` default.
  - `Redis::putTask()` still accepts no TTR: the adapter applies the timeout on
    pick, because `migrate()` only reaps rotten and delayed jobs on pop/pick.
- `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::work()` now treats `pickTask() === false` as
  an idle cycle, including when no work timeout is configured. Adapters must
  throw on real connection failures instead of returning `false`; Beanstalk
  transport failures now propagate to the worker.
- `BackQ\Adapter\Redis::ping()` returns `false` when the Redis connection
  check fails.
- `BackQ\Publisher\AbstractPublisher::ready()` is narrowed to `bool` (was
  untyped; it returned `null` before `start()`). The bound path is unchanged;
  callers and subclasses overriding `ready()` must use a `bool` return type.
- **The job id is acknowledged as a string.**
  `BackQ\Adapter\AbstractAdapter::afterWorkSuccess()` and
  `::afterWorkFailed()` take `?string $workId` (was `int|string|null`), and so do
  the three shipped adapters. The id a backend reports is its own: beanstalkd
  returns an `int` from `reserve()`, a `mysqli` row comes back as an `int` under
  `MYSQLI_OPT_INT_AND_FLOAT_NATIVE`, and a hand-written Redis payload may carry a
  number in its `id`. `pickTask()` is therefore left alone and keeps returning the
  backend's own type — `AbstractWorker` casts to a string where it invokes the two
  methods, so an adapter always receives a string.

  A subclass that overrides either method with `int|string|null` — or with any
  other type — **no longer loads**: PHP rejects a parameter type that is not
  `?string` exactly, in both directions. Match it and cast inside, where the
  implementation needs another form. Inside `Redis`, the id read out of a reserved
  payload is now a string before it reaches the `===` comparison against
  `$workId`, so a numeric `id` in a payload is acknowledged instead of rejected.
- `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::$workTimeout` is now typed `?int` (was untyped).
  Child classes that assign an `int` must declare `?int $workTimeout`.
- **The work timeout is a worker constructor argument and defaults to 60 seconds.**
  `AbstractWorker::__construct(AbstractAdapter $adapter, ?int $workTimeout = 60)`
  takes it, and `AbstractWorker::setWorkTimeout()` is `@deprecated` — still
  functional, so an existing worker keeps its pick cycle, and nothing is emitted at
  runtime: this library reports through PSR-3, and a PHP notice is not a channel it
  uses.

  | Was | Now |
  |---|---|
  | `$worker->setWorkTimeout(5);` | `new Serialized($adapter, workTimeout: 5);` |
  | `Serialized`, `Closure` and `AProcess` default to 5, `Guzzle` to 4, `GuzzleForwarder` to none | every worker defaults to 60 |

  A subclass that redeclared the property (`public ?int $workTimeout = 5;`) has to
  drop the declaration: the constructor assigns the value, so a redeclaration is
  dead code. `null` keeps the old behaviour of polling without blocking.

  Because a worker now always carries a timeout, **the idle-timeout check no longer
  throws**. `work()` used to raise `Time to pick next task cannot be lower than idle
  timeout` whenever `setIdleTimeout()` was not above the pick timeout — with a 60
  second default that would have been *every* `setIdleTimeout()` up to 60, including
  the ordinary "quit after 30 idle seconds". A work cycle may not outlast the idle
  deadline (the loop has to reach its idle check while the deadline is still ahead of
  it), so the timeout is now **lowered to `idleTimeout - 1`** instead, and both the
  adapter and the work loop use the lowered value — the two cannot disagree. The
  configured number is not rewritten, and the worker logs what it lowered and to what.
- `BackQ\Publisher\AbstractPublisher` takes the adapter through the constructor,
  mirroring `AbstractWorker`:
  - `__construct(?AbstractAdapter $adapter = null)` is now
    `__construct(AbstractAdapter $adapter)` — the adapter is required.
  - `setupAdapter()` is removed. Publishers that used to build their adapter in
    that override now receive it from the caller: `new MyPublisher($adapter)`.
    Implementations that need extra constructor arguments can still declare
    `__construct(AbstractAdapter $adapter, ...)` and call
    `parent::__construct($adapter)`.
  - `AbstractPublisher::$adapter` is now `protected` (was `private`) so a
    publisher can rebuild it after `unserialize()`.
  - `getInstance()` is `@deprecated`, triggers `E_USER_DEPRECATED` and throws
    `BadMethodCallException`: it can no longer build a publisher, because no
    adapter can be supplied without one.
  - `__wakeup()` is removed. The adapter is not serializable, so a publisher that
    travels through a queue (`BackQ\Message\Serialized` payloads) must restore it
    in its own `__wakeup()` — see `example/publishers/lib/*processpublisher.php`.
- Deprecated/dead API removed:
  - `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::debug()` (use `logDebug()`)
  - `BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk::pickTasks()` (dead, unused)
  - `BackQ\Adapter\AbstractAdapter::setTriggerErrorOnError()` and
    `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::setTriggerErrorOnError()`, together with the
    `triggerErrorOnError` property of both classes. `logError()` now reports to
    the PSR-3 logger only and no longer raises an `E_USER_WARNING`: remove the
    `setTriggerErrorOnError(false)` calls from your bootstrap, code that
    depended on the warning has to watch the logger instead. The
    `trigger_error()` calls of `BackQ\Worker\AProcess` and
    `BackQ\Worker\GuzzleForwarder` are unchanged.
- `BackQ\Message\Generic` keeps `Serializable` for backward compatibility but
  now also implements the native `__serialize()`/`__unserialize()` magic
  methods (removing the PHP 8.1+ interface deprecation). The legacy
  `serialize()`/`unserialize()` methods are `@deprecated`. Payloads produced by
  4.x (`C:` capsules) are still read back; new payloads serialize in the native
  `O:` format.
- Dependency floors raised (see `composer.json`): `symfony/console` is now a
  declared runtime dependency (was a transitive, undeclared one).
- `#[Override]` attributes are used on adapter, worker and message method
  overrides (PHP 8.3). Subclasses that override a method that already carries
  the attribute only need to keep their own signatures compatible — the
  attribute is not inherited and is not required on child overrides.
- Protocol reads no longer return truncated payloads:
  - `BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk\Client::_read($length)` (and therefore
    `reserve()`) throws a `RuntimeException` when the peer disconnects
    mid-body, instead of handing back the partial bytes as a complete job.
    `Beanstalk::pickTask()` already propagates transport failures.
  - `BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk\Client::_read($length)` strips exactly the 2-byte
    `\r\n` protocol terminator, so job bodies that legitimately end with
    `\r`, `\n` or `\r\n` are no longer silently truncated.
  - `BackQ\Adapter\IO\StreamIO::read()` throws a `TimeoutException`
    (`READ_EOF_CODE` / `READ_TIME_CODE`) on a short read instead of silently
    returning fewer than the requested number of bytes, and never reads past
    the requested length.

- **Acknowledging a job the queue no longer holds now reports `false`.** This is
  the one change here that turns a silent success into a visible error, and it
  is the reason to read this entry before anything else.

  | Adapter | Was | Now |
  |---|---|---|
  | `MySql` | `afterWorkSuccess()` / `afterWorkFailed()` ran an `UPDATE` and returned `0 <= $this->write($sql)`, and `write()` returns the affected-row count — so an id that matched no row, an already-acknowledged job, or one another worker held, all read as `true` | `false`, plus a `debug` line naming the method |
  | `Redis` | looked the id up in the in-process reserved set and, not finding it, fell through to `return true` — the queue was never told, and the caller was told it was | `false`, plus a `debug` line |

  `AbstractWorker` does inspect the answer, and it treats `false` as fatal: it
  throws `Exception('Worker failed to acknowledge job result')`, which the
  concrete worker's catch-all reports through PSR-3 at `error` and the cycle
  aborts. Until now a job lost to an expiry, a competing consumer or a restart
  was acknowledged as if it had been handled, and the worker's result was
  discarded silently. The `debug` line on the adapter is new output too, and a
  `debug`-level handler is unaffected by it. If your consumer is at-least-once by
  design and the duplicate work is cheaper than an aborted cycle, that is a real
  behavioural choice to make now — there is no per-adapter switch.

  `ping()`/`connect()` and `putTask()` are unchanged in this respect: a
  transport or storage failure is still **returned** as a `Throwable` rather
  than `false`, and an invalid argument still throws. `false` from an
  acknowledgement means "the backend did not confirm it", not "the call failed".
- **`BackQ\Adapter\Redis::__construct()` takes a value object instead of nine
  scalar parameters.** The signature was `__construct(LoggerInterface $logger,
  string $host, int $port, bool $persistent, ?int $persistent_id, ?string
  $prefix, int $timeout, int $read_timeout, int $database_id, ?string
  $auth_password)`; it is now `__construct(LoggerInterface $logger,
  RedisConfig $config = new RedisConfig())`. `BackQ\Adapter\Redis\RedisConfig` is
  a `readonly` class carrying the same nine settings plus the queue name:

  | Was | Now | Default unchanged |
  |---|---|---|
  | `$host` | `RedisConfig::$host` | `127.0.0.1` |
  | `$port` | `RedisConfig::$port` | `6379` |
  | `$persistent` | `RedisConfig::$persistent` | `false` |
  | `$persistent_id` | `RedisConfig::$persistentId` | `null` |
  | `$prefix` | `RedisConfig::$prefix` | `null` |
  | `$timeout` | `RedisConfig::$timeout` | `10` |
  | `$read_timeout` | `RedisConfig::$readTimeout` | `10` |
  | `$database_id` | `RedisConfig::$databaseId` | `0` |
  | `$auth_password` | `RedisConfig::$authPassword` | `null` |
  | *(none — see below)* | `RedisConfig::$queueName` | `default` |

  ```php
  // 4.x
  $adapter = new Redis($logger, '127.0.0.1', 6379, false, null, 'app:', 10, 10, 0, null);
  // 5.x
  $adapter = new Redis($logger, new RedisConfig(prefix: 'app:'));
  ```

  The new object validates what it holds and names the offending field:
  `port` must be 1–65535, `timeout` and `readTimeout` at least 1, and
  `databaseId` at least 0 — each an `InvalidArgumentException` at construction,
  where the old scalar signature accepted a bad value and failed later against
  the server.

  `queueName` is new rather than moved: in 4.x it was an untyped-default
  `private string $queueName` that only `bindRead()` / `bindWrite()` ever
  assigned, so it was uninitialized until one of them ran and the constructor
  could not name a queue at all. It is now a constructor setting defaulting to
  `default`, which is what an uninitialized read would have produced nothing for
  before. `bindRead()` / `bindWrite()` still overwrite it per call, so a
  per-role queue name behaves as it did.
- **`BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk::connect()` takes a value object, and its fifth
  `$logger` parameter is gone.** The signature was `connect(string $host =
  '127.0.0.1', int $port = 11300, int $timeout = 1, bool $persistent = false,
  $logger = null)`; it is now `connect(Connection $connection = new Connection())`.
  `BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk\Connection` is a `final readonly` class with `$host`,
  `$port` (validated 1–65535), `$timeout`, `$persistent` and a new `mixed
  $context`, plus `asPersistent()`:

  ```php
  // 4.x
  $adapter->connect('beanstalkd', 11300, 5, true);
  // 5.x
  $adapter->connect(new Connection(host: 'beanstalkd', timeout: 5, persistent: true));
  ```

  The dropped `$logger` existed only to be handed to `Beanstalk::error()`, which
  is itself **removed** — see below. Pass the logger to
  `AbstractAdapter::__construct()` as before; the adapter reports through it.
  The stream context is new and is what makes a TLS beanstalkd reachable:
  `new Connection(context: stream_context_create(['ssl' => ['cafile' => $ca]]))`.
- **`BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk::error()` is removed.** It was a public
  `error(string $msg): void` bridge so the vendored `davidpersson/beanstalk`
  client, which is configured with `'logger' => $adapter`, could report through
  the adapter's PSR-3 logger. The client no longer receives the adapter: it
  logs through `$this?->logger->error(...)` like every other adapter, so there
  is nothing to bridge. The client still receives a `'logger'` key in its
  config array and it is still `null` by default.
- **`PersistentBeanstalk` makes the connection persistent whatever the argument
  says.** `connect()` runs the `Connection` through a new
  `protected connection(Connection $connection): Connection` hook, and the
  subclass answers `$connection->asPersistent()` from it, so
  `new PersistentBeanstalk()->connect(new Connection(persistent: false))` is still
  a persistent connection — the class name is the intent, as it always was.
  Previously the flag on the argument decided it, which let a caller accidentally
  turn a persistent adapter into a non-persistent one. A subclass of
  `PersistentBeanstalk` that overrode `connect()` to alter the connection must
  override `connection()` instead, and the class is **not** `final`: subclassing it
  is still supported.
- **`BackQ\Adapter\MySql` is instantiable.** It was `abstract` with no abstract
  method of its own, so the only way to use it was a trivial subclass, and every
  third-party adapter in the wild exists for that reason. `new MySql($db, new
  JobConfig(), $logger)` is now the supported construction. The test double
  `BackQ\Tests\Support\TestMySqlAdapter` is deleted.
- **The MySQL queue's timestamps come from the database.** `pickTask()`,
  `putTask()` and the state update wrote `date('Y-m-d H:i:s')` interpolated into
  the SQL, so the job clock was the web server's clock and a second host could
  write a `time_sync` in the past. All three now write `NOW()`, which is the
  server's own clock. Code that set the PHP timezone to influence job ordering
  has no effect any more; set the session or server time zone instead.
- **A dead MySQL link is no longer the caller's problem by default.**
  `MySql::ping()` asks `mysqli::ping()` and reports `false`; it does **not**
  reconnect, because the driver cannot. `JobConfig` gained a seventh argument,
  `?\Closure $connectionProvider`, a factory returning a `mysqli`:

  ```php
  $config = new JobConfig(
      table: 'backq_jobs',
      connectionProvider: static fn (): mysqli => new mysqli('127.0.0.1', 'backq', 'secret', 'backq_jobs'),
  );
  ```

  `ping()` calls it **only** when `mysqli::ping()` reported the link dead, and
  **before** closing the dead link — a provider that throws leaves the caller the
  link it already has, so a failed reconnect is never worse than no reconnect.
  The dead link is then closed best-effort (a close failure is a `debug` line,
  not an error) and the new one takes its place. With no provider configured the
  behaviour is exactly as in 4.x: `ping()` reports `false`, logs why, and the
  adapter never closes a link it did not open. `ping(false)` never replaces the
  link whatever the provider does.
- **`trigger_error()` is gone from the Redis adapter.** Its illuminate
  `ExceptionHandler` bridge raised an `E_USER_WARNING` for every connection
  error, so a Redis outage produced one PHP warning per failed operation *and*
  a PSR-3 record. It now only `report()`s, and every other adapter already
  reported the same way. Code that watched the PHP error log for these messages
  must watch the PSR-3 logger — the same change 5.x already made for
  `AProcess`. The `trigger_error()` calls in
  `BackQ\Publisher\AbstractPublisher::getInstance()` and in
  `BackQ\Worker\AProcess` / `BackQ\Worker\GuzzleForwarder` are **unchanged**;
  they are a different concern and are not part of this release.
- **One failure policy, written once.** `AbstractAdapter` gained the
  `attempt()` / `attemptConnect()` / `attemptReturning()` / `attemptRethrowing()`
  family, so the "log it, return false, do not throw" decision an adapter used to
  repeat at each call site is now one method. It is protected: a subclass adapter
  that needs it calls `parent::`-style helpers rather than copying the try/catch.
  Behaviour is unchanged — a `Throwable` from a transport is returned, never
  thrown, and an `InvalidArgumentException` from your own validation still
  propagates. The log line is one PSR-3 record with `['exception' => $e]` in the
  context, where the `Beanstalk` adapter used to prefix its own text and the
  `Redis` one used to warn; a handler that matched on the message prefix has to
  match on the `exception` key instead.
- `BackQ\Adapter\Redis::STATE_NOTHING`, `STATE_BINDWRITE` and `STATE_BINDREAD`
  are `@deprecated`: `BackQ\Adapter\ConnectionState` is the supported way to name
  a connection state, and it gains a fourth case `Connected = 3` (the server
  answered, but no role has been picked yet) and an `isBound()` method. The
  three constants are kept and still hold `0`, `1` and `2`, so an existing
  integer comparison keeps working; they emit no runtime notice. The
  `AbstractWorker` / `AbstractPublisher` constructor parameter narrowing
  (`AbstractAdapter` → `QueueConsumer` / `QueueProducer`) is likewise
  **deferred to 6.0** and is not in this release — see the New Features entry
  for the two role interfaces.

========================================
2. New Features
========================================

- `BackQ\Adapter\ConnectionState`: backed `int` enum (`Nothing` = 0,
  `BindWrite` = 1, `BindRead` = 2) used internally by the `Redis`
  adapter for the connection state machine. The legacy `STATE_*` integer
  constants on the adapter are kept for backward compatibility.
- `BackQ\Message\Generic::$data` and `getData()` are typed (`mixed`).
- `BackQ\Worker\AbstractWorker::DEFAULT_WORK_TIMEOUT` (typed `int`, 60) names the
  default work timeout, so a caller can pass it on deliberately
  (`new Serialized($adapter, workTimeout: AbstractWorker::DEFAULT_WORK_TIMEOUT)`)
  or assert against it.
- `BackQ\Adapter\MySql`: new adapter that keeps the queue in a MySQL table
  instead of a queue server. It receives an established `mysqli` link through
  the constructor and never opens or closes it, so the application keeps
  control over the connection options, charset and pooling (needs the
  `ext-mysqli` extension). The job table and the throttling sleeps are passed
  as a `BackQ\Adapter\MySql\JobConfig` value object, the logger is the last
  argument, so a fully configured adapter is built with
  `new MySqlAdapter($db, new JobConfig(), $logger)`. Query failures surface as
  `mysqli_sql_exception`, the default error mode of the driver since PHP 8.1:
  `pickTask()` catches them, rolls the transaction back and returns `false`,
  while `putTask()` catches them, rolls the transaction back and returns the
  exception. `afterWorkSuccess()` and `afterWorkFailed()` report `false`
  instead of throwing, so an acknowledgement never breaks the worker loop.
- `BackQ\Adapter\MySql\JobState` and `BackQ\Adapter\MySql\JobColumn`: backed
  `string` enums for the job states of the `sync` column (`Wait` = `WAIT`,
  `Lock` = `LOCK`, `Done` = `DONE`, `Hold` = `HOLD`) and for the columns the
  adapter owns (`State` = `sync`, `Time` = `time_sync`). They replace the
  private `MySql::STATE_*` and `MySql::COLUMN_*` constants. The produced SQL did
  not change.
- `BackQ\Adapter\MySql\JobConfig`: a `final readonly` value object carrying the
  job columns, the job table and the three throttling sleeps that used to be
  the `$jobIdColumn`, `$jobDataColumn`, `$jobTable`, `$pickMissSleep`,
  `$pickSuccessSleep` and `$putTaskSleep` constructor arguments and properties
  of `MySql`. It is injected as the second constructor argument
  (`new MySqlAdapter($db, new JobConfig(), $logger)`). All defaults are
  unchanged. Subclasses that overrode the sleep properties now pass a
  `JobConfig` with their own values instead.
- `BackQ\Adapter\QueueConsumer` and `BackQ\Adapter\QueueProducer`: two small
  role interfaces. `QueueConsumer` is the four methods a worker needs (`ping`,
  `pickTask`, `afterWorkSuccess`, `afterWorkFailed`); `QueueProducer` is the
  four a publisher needs (`ping`, `putTask`, and the two it shares with the
  consumer). `AbstractAdapter` implements both, so every shipped adapter and
  every existing subclass is both roles with no change — but a consumer-only or
  producer-only component can now be typed against the role instead of the whole
  adapter, and a test double can implement four methods rather than eleven. The
  11 methods of `AbstractAdapter` are unchanged in name, signature and order, so
  `AbstractWorker` and `AbstractPublisher` still accept anything that extends it.

  ```php
  // A component that only publishes
  final class Mailer
  {
      public function __construct(private readonly QueueProducer $queue) {}
  }

  // A class that is not an AbstractAdapter at all
  final class InMemoryQueue implements QueueConsumer, QueueProducer { /* ... */ }
  ```

  Both roles are **deprecating** `AbstractAdapter` as the type to program to.
  6.0 is where `AbstractWorker::__construct()` and
  `AbstractPublisher::__construct()` narrow their parameter from
  `AbstractAdapter` to the matching role; a subclass of `AbstractAdapter` is
  unaffected by that, and a hand-rolled adapter that does not extend the abstract
  class will want `QueueConsumer` / `QueueProducer` instead.
- `BackQ\Adapter\Redis\RedisConfig`: the `final readonly` value object described
  in the Backward Incompatible Changes section above.
- `BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk\Connection`: the `final readonly` value object
  described above, with the stream context that makes TLS reachable and
  `asPersistent()`.
- `BackQ\Adapter\MySql\JobConfig::$connectionProvider`: a `\Closure(): \mysqli`
  factory the adapter calls when — and only when — `mysqli::ping()` has already
  reported the link dead. See above.
- `BackQ\Adapter\Beanstalk\Client` documents its delta from
  `davidpersson/beanstalk` in the class docblock: a nine-row table, one row per
  overridden method, saying what the vendored version does and what this one does
  instead. `BackQ\Tests\Adapter\Beanstalk\ClientTest` asserts the table against
  the code, so an override added or removed on either side fails the suite — a
  `composer update` of the fork is now a diff against a list rather than a
  mystery. That table is the only reason this class still carries
  `@phpcs:disable`; the other three adapter files that carried it
  (`Beanstalk.php`, `PersistentBeanstalk.php`, `IO/StreamIO.php`) are linted.
- `json_validate()` in place of `@json_decode(...)` + `is_array` checks.

